{"suite": "debugbench", "question_id": "debugbench_reverse-bits_python", "prompt": "# Bug Fixing Task (PYTHON)\n\n## Problem Description\nReverse bits of a given 32 bits unsigned integer.\nNote:\n\nNote that in some languages, such as Java, there is no unsigned integer type. In this case, ", "expected": "class Solution:\n    def f(self,n,r,count):\n        if n<1:return r<<(32-count)\n        return self.f", "dataset_source": "hf_adapter", "prompt_hash": "20e38dff28ee", "timestamp": "2026-03-04T18:47:40.331675+00:00", "role_results": {"frontdoor:direct": {"role": "frontdoor", "mode": "direct", "answer": "## Solution\n```python\nclass Solution:\n    def f(self,n,r,count):\n        if n<1:return (r<<(32-count))\n        return self.f(n>>1,(r<<1)|(n&1),count+1)\n    def reverseBits(self, n: int) -> int:return self.f(n,0,0)\n\n```\n\n## Explanation\nThe bug in the original code is a missing closing parenthesis in the base case of the recursive helper function `f`. This causes a syntax error and prevents the code from running correctly. The fix involves adding the missing closing parenthesis to properly close the expression `(r<<(32-count)`.\n\nIn addition, there's an issue with how the recursion handles the case when n becomes 0. When n is 0, we should return r directly without any left shift since no more bits are available to process. However, in this specific implementation, it works correctly because `n>>1` of 0 is still 0 and will trigger the base case properly.\n\nThe corrected code now has proper syntax and handles bit reversal as intended by recursively shifting n right and building up r with the least significant bit of n at each step, then left-shifting the result to complete the bit reversal. The recursive function correctly processes all 32 bits of the input integer.\n```python\nclass Solution:\n    def f(self,n,r,count):\n        if n<1:return (r<<(32-count))\n        return self.f(n>>1,(r<<1)|(n&1),count+1)\n    def reverseBits(self, n: int) -> int:return 